  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...As of 2:50 AM EDT...Current sfc analysis shows \nthe weak sfc low that brought showers yesterday departing well \nto our east. A trailing cold front is draped across the Mid \nHudson Valley, with a second, stronger front approaching NY and \nthe eastern Great Lakes from southern Canada. Ahead of this \nsecond front, abundant low-level moisture is leading to plenty \nof fog and low stratus. Dew points are also in the 60s, which is\nkeeping temperatures quite warm for mid September. Overnight \nlows will range from 50s (high terrain) to mid 60s (valleys). \n\nOnce the secondary cold front moves through this morning, the low \nclouds and fog should quickly mix out, with skies becoming partly to \nmostly clear. It will end up being a very pleasant day, with highs \nin the 60s (terrain) to mid 70s (valleys), lowering humidity, and \nbreezy N/NW winds. Winds diminish tonight as a 1028+ mb sfc high \nbuilds into southern Canada. Temperatures will drop into the 30s \n(terrain) to 40s (valleys). Despite the dry airmass, some patchy fog \nwill be possible in the typical river valleys and near wetlands. \nWith the ADKs being closest to the center of the sfc high, favorable \nradiational cooling conditions may allow for some patchy frost to \ndevelop in the sheltered areas, but coverage is not expected to be \nwidespread enough to warrant frost advisory issuance. \n\nTomorrow, the sfc high slides overhead. There will be some mid and \nhigh clouds around, but it should be a very nice early-fall day with \nhighs mainly in the 60s and continued low humidity. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Late Saturday night into Sunday, a sfc low will \ndevelop in response to an upper impulse and track along a stalled \nfrontal boundary from the Ohio Valley towards/just south of our \nregion. A strengthening LLJ will aid in transporting moisture \nnorthwards into our region. While the sfc warm front remains to our \nsouth, strong mid-level warm advection/isentropic lift/FGEN and the \nright entrance region of an upper jet streak to our north will \nprovide plenty of forcing for ascent, so we are expecting widespread \nrain and showers late Saturday night and Sunday. The heaviest rain \nwill fall across southern areas where the best mid-level forcing \nwill be. Total rainfall amounts generally look to be 0.25 to 0.75\" \nnorth of I-90, with 0.75 to 1.5\" south, except near I-84 where some \nhigher amounts up to around 2\" will be possible. Given that 1-hr FFG \nvalues are generally >2\", rain will be stratiform in nature, \nand little to no instability, we collaborated with neighboring \nWFOs and WPC to have the slight risk ERO trimmed back. However, \neven the northern extent of the marginal risk ERO still seems \ntoo bullish. While some nuisance ponding of water in the typical\nlow-lying and poor drainage areas is possible, the bottom line \nis that the probability of any flash flooding in the ALY CWA is \nvery, very low at this time. Due to widespread rain/wetbulbing, \nhighs Sunday may not make it out of the 50s for many areas. \n\nShowers should come to an end Sunday night as a strengthening sfc \nhigh builds into southeastern Canada beneath confluent upper flow. \nThis will help push the frontal boundary back south, so Monday looks \nrelatively dry at this time. For Tuesday through the middle of next \nweek, there is some uncertainty if the sfc high will continue to \nsuppress this boundary to our south or if the sfc high will retreat \nenough that the boundary is able to lift north and bring some \nrenewed shower chances to our southern areas. Given the strength of \nthe sfc high, we are leaning towards the drier solution. With our \narea expected to be on the cool side of the frontal boundary, temps \nshould remain below normal with highs in the 50s (terrain) to 60s \n(elsewhere) and lows in the upper 30s to 40s each day/night.",
